// ==================================================================
//  A SOCIETY OF PIECE-AGENTS THAT PLAYS CHESS
// ==================================================================
//
//  Companion listing to "Sixteen Scientists on One Board". Every construction
//  of the note appears here as ordinary code:
//
//    Sec. 5    the board        -- Board, Cells, Settle, Ray  (three families)
//    Sec. 5.3  legality         -- Legal, Pins                (the world's ALL)
//    Sec. 6    the ply is a join-- Ply                        (N-ary, atomic)
//    Sec. 7    coordination     -- Franchise, Advocate, Gate  (quorum = arity)
//    Sec. 7.6  rounds           -- Clock                      (+ last resort)
//    Sec. 8    coalition rung   -- Ladder rung 6              (//! ideal)
//    Sec. 9    lookahead        -- Check, Shadow              (reflection)
//    Sec. 10   material         -- Harvest, Live              (conservation)
//    Sec. 10.1 the treasury     -- Treasury, Disburse         (a source)
//
//  CONVENTION. A parameter written `c` is a NAME (a channel); a parameter
//  written `@d` is DATA. To pass a channel one sends `*c`, since @(*c) = c.
//
//  NOTATION. Guards are written in the generated spatial-behavioural logic
//  extended with the coalition modality: out(n)phi, in(n)phi, phi | psi, the
//  name predicate @phi, the context-labelled modality <K_m>phi, its settled
//  form <<K_m>>phi, the greatest fixed point nu X. phi, graded separation
//  phi |_k^N over a DESCRIBED namespace N, and <E>_N^V psi over opens of N.
//  There is no hidden-name quantifier and no need of one: `new` is syntactic
//  sugar, since a name is a quoted process and no process contains the name
//  that codes it, so a name fresh in P is manufactured by quoting any term
//  properly containing P. Every family below is manufactured that way, which
//  is what makes it DESCRIBABLE and hence what makes |_k^N and <E>_N^V apply.
//  The current reducer accepts only the arithmetic and pattern fragment of
//  `where`; guards using a structural, modal or coalition connective are
//  marked `//! ideal` and specify a check rather than perform one.
//
//  LOOKAHEAD IS REFLECTION (Sec. 9). A naive reading of this architecture
//  says search is beyond it: sixteen budget-bounded agents, a rendezvous
//  price per candidate, no central evaluator, in a game that rewards depth.
//  That reading ignores what the context-labelled modalities are for. The
//  modalities are not an evaluation device standing beside search; they ARE
//  the search, and modal depth is ply depth. <<K_m>>phi requires the ply to
//  FIRE, so evaluating a modal formula means exhibiting the rewrite -- which
//  the checker does on a SHADOW BOARD @[*chk,H,d,i], manufactured by quoting
//  its own channel together with a handle on the deposit. It may drive that
//  without restriction because it is its own term, and by freshness-by-quotation
//  quotation it cannot collide with the live board. Imagining a position is
//  quoting the world and driving the copy.
//
//  With the shared search memory of Sec. 9.5 the society is recognisably a
//  loosely-coupled parallel search over a common record (Obs. 9.17), so the
//  advocacy protocol is a CONSTANT FACTOR per turn and not an exponent
//  (Cor. 9.18). What makes this player weak is elsewhere: the leaf evaluation
//  below, and the fact that a quorum aggregates by PLURALITY where an engine
//  aggregates by MAXIMUM.
//
//  THE THREE FAMILIES (Sec. 5.1). Square s of board b carries:
//      occ(s) = @[*b,"occ",s]        occupancy; the ONLY family a ply consumes
//      atk(s,c,p) = @[*b,"atk",s,c,p]  deposited attack markers; read-only
//      pin(s) / chk(c) / ep(s) / cast(c,side)   deposited constraints
//  Were attack markers deposited on occ, a scan signalling "p bears on s"
//  could rendezvous with a move protocol receiving on occ(s) and the scan
//  would PLAY A PLY. Perception must not be able to act; the separation of
//  access grades one and two is enforced here by a choice of namespace, and
//  with distinct families the failure is UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely
//  avoided by discipline.
//
//  CONSERVATION. Nothing mints. The Treasury holds an opening reserve Theta_W
//  that is part of Theta and is never replenished; its schedule redistributes
//  a fixed endowment over time. Franchise issues one LINEAR advocacy token per
//  live piece per round and does not carry it over. Harvest MOVES a captured
//  agent's stack into the capturer's. Every Live debit moves a token from
//  sigma into kappa. So sigma + kappa = Theta throughout.
// ==================================================================

new stdout(`rho:io:stdout`), display, arena, wall, player, clock,
    Live, Treasury, Disburse, Board, Cells, Ray, Settle, Pins, Legal, Ply, Harvest,
    Registry, Franchise, Piece, Candidates, Evaluate, Advocate, Gate,
    Ladder, Revise, Check, Turn, Round, Adjudicate, Boot
in {

  for (@line <= display) { stdout!(line) }

  // ==================================================================
  //  METABOLISM  --  reclaim, debit, reissue.
  //  Between the receipt and the send the stack is a message in flight: living
  //  is racing others to your own stack, and the metabolic channel m is
  //  exactly the vulnerability that Harvest exploits when a piece is captured.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Live(m, @burn, step) = {
      for (@sigma <- m) {
        match sigma > burn {
          true  => { m!(sigma - burn) | *step | Live!(*m, burn, *step) }
          false => { display!(("DIED", "could not fund a step")) }
        }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE TREASURY  --  a source, not a prey.  Sec. 10.1, Def. 10.5.
  //
  //  Rate-limited, non-exclusive, and NON-ADAPTIVE -- it does not read the
  //  position. That access profile is what makes it a SOURCE rather than a
  //  JUDGE, and it is the whole of Prin. 10.9: a disbursement rule that read
  //  position quality would be an evaluation function held by the treasury,
  //  agents would be paid for agreeing with it, and the yield measurement
  //  would degenerate into measuring how well the ladder tracks the
  //  exchequer. That is the Rem. 5.5 leak arriving through the ACCOUNTS
  //  rather than through the floor, where it is much harder to see.
  //
  //  CONSERVATION. Theta_W is fixed at the outset and never replenished, so
  //  the schedule REDISTRIBUTES a fixed endowment over time and does not
  //  mint: a large initial supply, meagrely distributed at first and
  //  increasingly as play goes on. sigma + kappa = Theta throughout.
  //
  //  THE SCHEDULE IS KEYED TO CAPTURED MATERIAL, not to ply number. An
  //  exponential in ply diverges against a finite reserve, and one then
  //  harvests draws by exhaustion that are artefacts of the treasury rather
  //  than facts about the players. Captured material is bounded, monotone and
  //  self-normalising, and it says something better: the treasury pays out in
  //  proportion to how much of the position has been RESOLVED, so a simpler
  //  position funds a deeper search (Rem. 10.6).
  //
  //  WHY THIS EXISTS. Harvest-only funding is PROCYCLICAL: the side that is
  //  behind has fewer agents and smaller stacks, so by Prin. 9.3 it calculates
  //  less far and falls further behind. That spiral is a CHOSEN property of a
  //  funding rule, not a structural feature of the architecture, and this
  //  contract is the correction. Note the convergence with Prop. 7.10: S falls
  //  as q rises, so per-agent budget rises SUPER-LINEARLY, and the endgame is
  //  where the society is simultaneously split-free by arithmetic and deepest
  //  by budget. Cor. 10.8: this player should be at its BEST in the endgame,
  //  the opposite of how weak engines usually fail, and therefore cheap to
  //  falsify (M11).
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Treasury(t, @captured, @reserve) = {
      for (@ids, ret <- t) {
        new q in {
            q!(Schedule(captured))          // non-decreasing in captured
          | for (@rate <- q) {
              new draw in {
                  draw!(rate * Size(ids))
                | for (@total <- draw) {
                    match reserve >= total {
                      true  => {   Disburse!(ids, rate, *ret)
                                 | Treasury!(*t, captured, reserve - total) }
                      // Exhausted. The reserve is finite, and this is what the
                      // end of a finite endowment looks like; it is not a
                      // failure mode to be patched away.
                      false => { ret!(0) | Treasury!(*t, captured, 0) }
                    }
                  }
              }
            }
        }
      }
    }

  // ASSAY-CONFIRMED DISBURSEMENT  --  Def. 10.10, Prin. 10.11.
  //
  //  The schedule above sets the FLOOR and removes the spiral; it pays per
  //  surviving agent regardless of how that agent is playing. This rule
  //  supplies the DIFFERENTIAL, and it is where selection lives. It reads only
  //  the confirm / refute / bottom trichotomy the assay ALREADY emits -- the
  //  same observable Revise uses to set its temperature -- so the judge is
  //  endogenous and no exogenous score is consulted.
  //
  //  Under this rule a hypothesis that pays for itself BUYS ITS OWN DEPTH, and
  //  one that does not starves its holder. "Is the yield of a hypothesis worth
  //  its price" stops being a statistic computed after the games and becomes
  //  the mechanism by which the population changes. Build and measure it
  //  SEPARATELY from the schedule (Rem. 10.12): the first is a correction to a
  //  defect, the second is an experiment.
  //
  //  NB the peek. Reading an agent's assay verdict must not consume it, for
  //  the same reason the settling peeks at occupancy: paying for an outcome
  //  must not destroy the record of it.
  | contract Disburse(@ids, @rate, ret) = {
      match ids {
        [] => { ret!("done") }
        [id ...rest] => {
            for (@verdict <<- @[*player,"assay",id]) {      // PEEK
              match verdict {
                "confirm" => { for (@sg <- @[*board,"m",id]) {
                                 @[*board,"m",id]!(sg + rate) } }
                _         => { Nil }        // refuted or bottom: no draw
              }
            }
          | Disburse!(rest, rate, *ret)
        }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE BOARD  --  occupancy only. Note what is NOT here: a `new` for the
  //  squares. Each square name is the quote of a term properly containing the
  //  board, so by the no-self-code theorem it cannot already occur in the
  //  board -- locally fresh, no registry consulted, decentralised by
  //  construction. And because the family is generated by a predicate on
  //  names, the whole namespace can be DESCRIBED rather than enumerated.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Board(b, @placement) = { Cells!(*b, placement, 0) | Settle!(*b) }

  | contract Cells(b, @placement, @s) = {
      match s < 64 {
        true  => {   @[*b,"occ",s]!(placement.nth(s))
                   | Cells!(*b, placement, s + 1) }
        false => { Nil }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE SETTLING  --  Sec. 5.2 and Rem. 5.3.
  //
  //  A ray walk PEEKS at occupancy (`<<-`) and never consumes it. That is what
  //  makes the scan perception rather than interaction: with a consuming
  //  receipt the settling would move the pieces by looking at them, and
  //  sixteen agents scanning concurrently would serialise on every square.
  //
  //  Termination: each step consumes one square of a strictly decreasing
  //  remaining ray. Confluence: markers are deposited on the atk family, which
  //  nothing in the settling reads. That discharges the obligation <<K_m>>
  //  depends on, which in noughts was Lem. 8 and here is harder because the
  //  walk is recursive in occupancy.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Ray(b, @from, @dir, @p, @c, @dist) = {
      match (dist > 0) and Onboard(from, dir) {
        true  => {
          new here in {
            here!(Step(from, dir)) |
            for (@s <- here) {
              for (@v <<- @[*b,"occ",s]) {          // PEEK, never consume
                  @[*b,"atk",s,c,p]!(("bears", from))
                | match v {
                    Nil => { Ray!(*b, s, dir, p, c, dist - 1) }  // ray continues
                    _   => { Nil }                              // blocked here
                  }
              }
            }
          }
        }
        false => { Nil }
      }
    }

  // Every occupied square emits its piece's rays; then the kings emit theirs
  // to deposit pins. Rem 5.4: this terminates and is confluent.
  | contract Settle(b) = {
      new each in {
          Squares!(*b, *each)
        | for (@s <= each) {
            for (@v <<- @[*b,"occ",s]) {
              match v {
                Nil => { Nil }
                _   => { Directions!(v, s, *b) }   // per-type ray emission
              }
            }
          }
        | Pins!(*b)
      }
    }

  // A pin marker is deposited by walking OUT from a king through the first own
  // piece to the first enemy slider on the same ray. Pin-awareness is
  // therefore PERCEPTION: a piece does not reason about whether it is pinned,
  // it reads whether a marker stands on its square. This is the note's best
  // instance of read-versus-drive (Sec. 5.3).
  | contract Pins(b) = {
      new k in {
          Kings!(*b, *k)
        | for (@(ksq, c) <= k) {
            new d in {
                Rays8!(*d)
              | for (@dir <= d) { PinWalk!(*b, ksq, dir, c, Nil) }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  A PLY IS A JOIN  --  Sec. 6, Def. 6.1 and Prop. 6.2.
  //
  //  One N-ary join over the occupancy cells the move touches, guarded on the
  //  constraint family, re-emitting every consumed cell with its post-move
  //  payload. A failed guard leaves the messages in the tuple space, which is
  //  the platform's own semantics for a failed `where`, so a refusal is a
  //  no-op and not a rollback.
  //
  //  ARITY IS THE MOVE TAXONOMY:
  //     2  quiet move        (*p, Nil)        |-> (Nil, *p)
  //     2  capture           (*p, *q)         |-> (Nil, *p), q terminated
  //     3  en passant        (*p, Nil, *q)    |-> (Nil, *p, Nil), q terminated
  //     4  castling          (*k,Nil,Nil,*r)  |-> (Nil, *k, *r, Nil)
  //     2  promotion         (*p, .)          |-> (Nil, *p'), p retired
  //
  //  This is what keeps the redex positions of the ply rule in bijection with
  //  the legal moves, and hence what keeps <K_m> a GENERATED modality rather
  //  than a derived abbreviation (Cor. 6.3).
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Ply(b, @m, ret) = {
      match Kind(m) {

        // --- quiet move: arity 2 -------------------------------------------
        "quiet" => {
          for ( @vf <- @[*b,"occ",From(m)] & @vt <- @[*b,"occ",To(m)]
                where Legal(m, vf, vt) ) {                        //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",From(m)]!(Nil)
            | @[*b,"occ",To(m)]!(vf)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // --- capture: arity 2, and the occupant is EATEN --------------------
        //  Harvest routes the captured agent's metabolic name to the capturer.
        //  The arena mints nothing; predation is legal here for precisely one
        //  reason, and it is that somebody took a piece.
        "capture" => {
          for ( @vf <- @[*b,"occ",From(m)] & @vt <- @[*b,"occ",To(m)]
                where Legal(m, vf, vt) ) {                        //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",From(m)]!(Nil)
            | @[*b,"occ",To(m)]!(vf)
            | Harvest!(Stack(vt), Stack(vf), *display)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // --- castling: arity 4, all-or-nothing ------------------------------
        //  The same shape as the four-cell SWAP of the cost-accounting demo:
        //  the guard decides BEFORE any leg fires, so no interleaving can
        //  leave the rook moved and the king not.
        "castle" => {
          for ( @vk <- @[*b,"occ",KFrom(m)] & @v1 <- @[*b,"occ",KTo(m)]
              & @v2 <- @[*b,"occ",RTo(m)]   & @vr <- @[*b,"occ",RFrom(m)]
                where CastleLegal(m, vk, v1, v2, vr) ) {          //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",KFrom(m)]!(Nil) | @[*b,"occ",KTo(m)]!(vk)
            | @[*b,"occ",RFrom(m)]!(Nil) | @[*b,"occ",RTo(m)]!(vr)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // en passant (arity 3) and promotion follow the same pattern.
        _ => { ret!(("refused", m)) }
      }
    }

  // Conservation: nothing minted, the loser's stack MOVED into the winner's.
  | contract Harvest(mLost, mWon, ret) = {
      for (@a <- mLost & @c <- mWon) { mWon!(a + c) | ret!(("ate", a)) }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE FRANCHISE  --  Sec. 7.4. One LINEAR advocacy token per live piece per
  //  round, into a located stack whose channel is manufactured from the
  //  piece's identity. This is the polity listing's pre-election phase
  //  verbatim: a persistent claim on the roll metered to one linear token per
  //  epoch. It is a SOURCE keyed to identity, not a mint.
  //
  //  It is also what makes Thm. 7.6 arithmetic rather than statistical.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Franchise(@ids, @r) = {
      match ids {
        [] => { Nil }
        [id ...rest] => {
            @[*player,"V",id,r]!(("vote", id, r))
          | Franchise!(rest, r)
        }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  A PIECE  --  Sec. 12.3, steps 4-6 of the turn protocol.
  //
  //  A piece enumerates ITS OWN moves from ITS OWN square. Nobody computes the
  //  union of candidates; the union is what the advocacy namespace IS.
  //  Evaluation is an ASSAY: the rung sits in guard position, so evaluating is
  //  testing and confirmation is the rendezvous. By Prop. 17 of the scientist
  //  note the number of candidates a piece can afford to evaluate is its stack
  //  divided by the price of one check, so a poor piece does not survey its
  //  own branching -- and since DEPTH is bought from the same stack, a poor
  //  piece does not look far either.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Piece(b, @id, @rung, @r, m) = {
      new cand, best in {
          Candidates!(*b, id, *cand)
        | for (@c <- cand where Sat(c, rung, id)) {               //! ideal
            best!(c)
          }
        | for (@c <- best) { Advocate!(id, c, r) }
      }
    }

  // Spending the token IS the advocacy. A candidate move c is a term, so @c is
  // a name; the advocacy channel is manufactured from it. An OPEN in the
  // coalition sense is the set of pieces that have advocated for a given move
  // this round -- described by a name predicate, never enumerated.
  | contract Advocate(@id, @c, @r) = {
      for (@tok <- @[*player,"V",id,r]) {          // LINEAR: spent once
        @[*player,"adv",@c]!(id)
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE COMMIT GATE  --  Sec. 7.5, Def. 7.5.
  //
  //  QUORUM IS A JOIN ARITY. The gate is a k-ary join on one advocacy channel;
  //  it fires exactly when k distinct advocacy sends stand there, and it
  //  CONSUMES them. Shown for k = 3; the general case is generated.
  //
  //  THM 7.6. Two gates on distinct candidates consume 2k tokens drawn from a
  //  pool of S linear tokens, so two quorums require S >= 2k. With S = 16 and
  //  k >= 9 a SPLIT IS STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, for any policies whatever, and
  //  no cross-inhibition machinery is needed to secure it. Safety is free;
  //  liveness is not, and that is what Round is for.
  //
  //  THE PRICE OF THE GOVERNANCE LAYER. An engine takes the MAXIMUM over its
  //  candidates; a quorum takes a PLURALITY above a threshold. So the society
  //  can beat its average member and will struggle to beat its best one, and
  //  a piece holding the right move loses to eight pieces holding a worse one.
  //  That is the architecture, not a defect of it -- a maximum over sixteen
  //  agents needs an evaluator none of them can afford to be. But do NOT read
  //  it as an established structural weakness. Against the commit gap runs a
  //  COVERAGE GAIN the same architecture supplies: sixteen agents at
  //  heterogeneous depths over different candidate sets examine a wider tree
  //  than any one of them could (Obs. 9.17 read as a benefit rather than an
  //  overhead). Which dominates is not known and is not obvious in either
  //  direction; M10 nets the two, and Q12 asks what can be bought back
  //  without reintroducing a chairman.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Gate(@c, @k, ret) = {
      for ( @a1 <- @[*player,"adv",@c]
          & @a2 <- @[*player,"adv",@c]
          & @a3 <- @[*player,"adv",@c] ) {
        @[*player,"commit"]!(@c) | ret!(("quorum", @c, [a1,a2,a3]))
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE ROUND  --  Sec. 7.6, Def. 7.8 and Def. 7.9.
  //
  //  Unconsumed advocacy is LEFT STANDING and is readable, so a piece may
  //  re-advocate having PERCEIVED where the rest of the society stood. That is
  //  the bees reading dance strength at the cluster: cheap perceptual price for
  //  the reversible decision, expensive interaction price for the irreversible
  //  one. Cross-inhibition is deliberately absent -- it is one more mechanism
  //  and the swarm measurement recorded that it suppresses CORRECT advocacy
  //  too. Deadlock is broken by re-advocacy and, failing that, by the clock.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Round(b, @ids, @r, @maxR, ret) = {
      new fired in {
          Franchise!(ids, r)
        | Pieces!(*b, ids, r)
        | Gates!(ids, r, *fired)
        | for (@res <- fired) { ret!(res) }
        | for (@t <- Timeout(r)) {                                //! ideal
            match r < maxR {
              true  => { Round!(*b, ids, r + 1, maxR, *ret) }
              // Resolver of last resort: the CLOCK, not a piece. It holds no
              // evaluation function and no preferences; its firing rate is a
              // measurement (M4), because a society that reaches it often is a
              // society whose quorum parameters are wrong.
              false => { MostAdvocated!(ids, r, *ret) }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE LADDER  --  Sec. 12.4. Cumulative, highest priority first. The rung
  //  index is the modal depth of the conjunct it adds, hence the ultrametric
  //  stage: d(Phi_r, Phi_{r+1}) = 2^-r. Prices are kappa(d) = beta^d per
  //  candidate examined -- and for the modal rungs that is DERIVED, not
  //  stipulated: it is the cost of instantiating a shadow board and driving
  //  it d plies (Prop. 9.8). The one free parameter is beta.
  //
  //  Note where the alternation is. Rungs 1, 2 and 5 have no modality at all:
  //  capturing, not hanging, and centralising are PERCEPTION, reading the
  //  markers the last settling deposited. The one genuinely alternating rung
  //  is 4, a box under a diamond, which by Prop. 6 of the scientist note has
  //  no finite confirming witness. In noughts that rung was also, measurably,
  //  the worst purchase on the ladder. Whether that repeats in chess is M2 --
  //  but rung 4 in noughts is a two-ply search on a 5478-position game and
  //  rung 4 here is a two-ply search on CHESS, so the ratio must be taken at
  //  MATCHED DEPTH or it compares different instruments.
  //
  //  The rung fixes the SHAPE of what an agent asks; its STACK fixes how far.
  //  Depth is therefore endogenous and per-agent (Prin. 9.3): an agent that
  //  has eaten calculates further than one that has not. NB this is a CHOSEN
  //  dial, not a structural property -- depth could be funded from a per-turn
  //  allowance instead, and Sec. 14 says so.
  //
  //  WHAT THIS LADDER IS. It is an evaluation function, and a thin one. That
  //  is a SHORT LADDER, not a weak logic: the generated logic has nu, graded
  //  separation over described namespaces, the name predicate and
  //  redex-indexed modalities, and under reflection reaches further again. An
  //  earlier draft called the coarseness forced on the grounds that a rung
  //  must be a formula of the logic; that confused expressibility with length.
  //
  //  WHAT ACTUALLY GOVERNS IS PRICE, and price turns on what the world has
  //  DEPOSITED. Rungs 5a and 5b below are the demonstration: king safety and
  //  pawn structure are classically computed per evaluated position, and
  //  deposited by the settling walk they are depth-ONE reads. This is
  //  read-versus-drive (Sec. 5.2) applied to EVALUATION rather than to
  //  legality, which is why M7 is a larger experiment than it first looks --
  //  the question is not merely who pays for the attack map, but how much of
  //  an evaluation function the world can be made to compute once for all.
  //
  //  WHAT IS STILL OPEN IS FINDABILITY (Q11): not whether the logic can say a
  //  good evaluation, nor whether an agent can afford one, but whether the
  //  revision process can FIND one. Confinement says an incremental walk never
  //  leaves its initial ball, and choosing the SHAPE of an evaluation is a
  //  distance-2^-1 move, so no individual gets there -- which leaves the
  //  population and its crossover operator as the only route.
  // ==================================================================

  //! ideal
  //  Emp(s)        := out(occ(s))@Nil
  //  Bears(p,s)    := exists c. out(atk(s,c,p)) TOP
  //  Fork(p)       := Threat(p) |_0^{N_atk} Threat(p)
  //  Bearing(s)    := <E>_{N_pl}^V  lambda p. Bears(p,s) (x) Available(p)
  //  KingSafe(c)   := <E>_{N_atk}^V lambda p. exists s. kz(c,s) and
  //                                 Bears(p,s) and Colour(p, not c)
  //  Worsens(m,Pawns) := <<K m>> PawnDebt > PawnDebt
  //  <<K_m>>phi    := the ply join at m fires, the board settles, phi holds
  | contract Ladder(@r, ret) = {
      match r {
        0 => { ret!( @{ TOP } ) }
        1 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m)) } ) }
        2 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m) } ) }
        3 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me) } ) }
        4 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them) } ) }
        5 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them)
                                  or Central(to(m)) or Develops(m) } ) }
        // 5a, 5b: depth-ONE reads over deposited structure, Def. 12.3 and
        // 12.4. kz(c,s) is one extra walk in the settling, over a
        // neighbourhood of bounded size, in the pass that already walks every
        // ray; pwn(c,f,kind) is a multiplicity condition on one file and
        // negations over the adjacent ones. Neither needs a search and
        // neither needs more logic than the ladder already used.
        51 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them)
                                  or Central(to(m)) or Develops(m)
                                  or <<K m>> KingSafe(me) } ) }
        52 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them)
                                  or Central(to(m)) or Develops(m)
                                  or <<K m>> KingSafe(me)
                                  or not Worsens(m, Pawns) } ) }
        // The FORCING rung, Def. 9.10. <E> does not mean "this coalition has
        // the resources"; it means the coalition can FORCE the goal whatever
        // the complement does -- and the complement is the OPPONENT'S pieces.
        // So the opponent enters as the complement of a coalition rather than
        // as a second search agent to be simulated, and rung 4 is this rung at
        // fixed-point depth one with a weaker target (Prop. 9.11).
        // Attained under (x) = min, so Check returns the WITNESS: at depth 0
        // the coalition, at depth d the LINE.
        6 => { ret!( @{ exists m. exists s. <<K m>>
                        <E>_{N_pl}^V nu X. (Mate(s) (+) <K> X) } ) }
        // chi is listed for completeness. Unlike noughts it is NOT exact at any
        // finite unfolding, and by Rem. 3.1 that is exactly the condition under
        // which the ecology does not die of its own perfection.
        7 => { ret!( @{ nu X. forall m. [K m](not Lost
                                and exists m2. <<K m2>> X) } ) }
      }
    }

  // Revision walks the lattice. The move has the two parameters the metric
  // grades: a LOCUS and a DIRECTION. Refutation rate is the temperature, and it
  // is an observable the assays already emit, not a schedule imposed from
  // outside. NB no case touches the piece's ADVOCACY STYLE: that is the
  // stage-1 approximant, a move of distance 2^-1, and an individual can
  // neither reach it by deepening nor afford it if it could. It changes across
  // generations, by crossover, and that is where a model-backed mutation
  // operator earns its place (Sec. 11.2).
  | contract Revise(@rung, @refutes, @confirms, ret) = {
      match refutes * 4 > confirms {
        true  => { ret!(rung + 1) }
        false => { ret!(rung) }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE CHECKER  --  Sec. 12.6. An instrument the player HOLDS, sited in the
  //  world so that sixteen agents share one cache. It receives only on the
  //  deposited families and NEVER on occ: otherwise the pride would move the
  //  zebra by looking at it. Verdicts are Kleene {f < b < t} with b carrying
  //  both readings -- the environment is stuck, or the observer could not
  //  afford to decide -- and the caller must not be able to tell which.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Check(@formula, @posHandle, @algebra, @depth, payer, ret) = {
      new r in {
          // Price is kappa(d) = beta^d SHADOW PLIES, not a stipulated
          // constant: Prop. 9.8. beta is measured, not set, and it is exactly
          // what a pruning discipline reduces.
          Live!(*payer, Price(formula, depth), *r)
        | for (_ <- r) {
            match Modal(formula) {
              // Depth 0: read the deposited families. PERCEPTION.
              false => { Evaluate!(formula, posHandle, algebra, *ret) }
              // Depth d: quote the deposit, manufacture a private board, and
              // drive it. REFLECTION. The witness at depth d is a PRINCIPAL
              // VARIATION (Prop. 9.13); at depth 0 it is a coalition.
              true  => { Shadow!(posHandle, depth, 0, formula, algebra, *ret) }
            }
          }
        // If Live cannot fund the query the piece receives nothing, which the
        // protocol reads as verdict "b" -- here in its search-shaped form, "I
        // ran out of depth", which is indistinguishable from "the position is
        // genuinely unresolved". Two bottoms, one value, by design.
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE SHADOW BOARD  --  Sec. 9.2, Def. 9.5 and Req. 9.6.
  //
  //  REQ 9.6. The checker may not move THE board: on the live board it PEEKS
  //  (`<<-`) and never consumes, on any family. It may receive freely on the
  //  families of a board it has MANUFACTURED. A shadow name is the quote of a
  //  term properly containing the checker, so by the no-self-code theorem it
  //  is fresh in the live board and the two families CANNOT collide. The
  //  separation is structural, not disciplinary -- the same argument that put
  //  attack markers on their own family in Sec. 5.1, one level up.
  //
  //  Each ply driven here is one metered step, so the recursion IS the search
  //  tree and Prop. 9.8 reads off its price. The alternation is fixed by the
  //  formula; the ORDER in which candidates are examined is not, which is why
  //  pruning is a scheduler discipline and not a different formula (Obs 9.12).
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Shadow(@H, @d, @i, @formula, @algebra, ret) = {
      new sb in {
          sb!(@[*Check, H, d, i])          // manufactured, hence fresh
        | for (@b2 <- sb) {
            match d {
              0 => { Evaluate!(formula, b2, algebra, *ret) }
              _ => {
                new cand, acc in {
                    Board!(b2, Placement(H))   // instantiate the copy
                  | ShadowMoves!(b2, *cand)
                  | for (@m <= cand) {
                      new r2 in {
                          Ply!(b2, m, *r2)     // the COPY may be moved
                        | for (@o <- r2) {
                            Shadow!(Deposit(b2), d - 1, i + 1,
                                    Peel(formula), algebra, *acc)
                          }
                      }
                    }
                    // Over an attained algebra (tropical, Viterbi) the (+) is
                    // realised by a definable selection, so Combine returns
                    // the WITNESS LINE and not merely its value. A pruning
                    // discipline is sound exactly when it leaves that witness
                    // alone (Cor. 9.14) -- so a changed principal variation
                    // under pruning is a soundness bug, not noise.
                  | Combine!(*acc, algebra, *ret)
                }
              }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE ARENA  --  the rules, holding no stack of its own. It mints nothing;
  //  it ROUTES. An exogenous reward would need a referee with a mint, and a
  //  referee with a mint destroys conservation.
  //
  //  It is also where the one unavoidable universal lives. Check, pin and
  //  discovered check constrain the legality of EVERY piece's move; no piece
  //  can establish that locally and no quorum can establish it either, because
  //  an illegal move is not a move and a vote does not make it one. So the
  //  world refuses, and the player's own hypothesis language stays existential
  //  and finitely confirmable.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Turn(b, @ids, @maxR, @who, over) = {
      new chosen in {
          Round!(*b, ids, 1, maxR, *chosen)
        | for (@res <- chosen) {
            new played in {
                Ply!(*b, Move(res), *played)
              | for (@out <- played) {
                  match out {
                    ("played", m) => {
                        Adjudicate!(*b, *over, who)
                      | Turn!(*b, Live_ids(ids), maxR, 3 - who, *over)
                    }
                    // A refused guard left the messages in the tuple space, so
                    // the position is untouched and the clock simply retries.
                    ("refused", m) => { Turn!(*b, ids, maxR, who, *over) }
                  }
                }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  //! ideal -- mate, stalemate and the draw conditions are modal formulae over
  //  the deposited families, not scans of a list. The fifty-move and repetition
  //  rules are the only genuinely HISTORICAL facts in chess and nothing here
  //  uses the history monad. That is open problem Q4, and it is now the
  //  largest item in the design: a SEARCHED fact -- this deposit was driven
  //  to depth d and returned v -- is a configuration paired with the word of
  //  events that produced it, which is the history monad's own object. The
  //  transposition table is its ecological reading, and the duplication it
  //  removes grows with beta^d rather than with S (Sec. 9.5).
  | contract Adjudicate(b, over, @who) = {
      new r in {
          @[*b,"state"]!(*r)
        | for (@pos <- r where pos |= @{ Mate(3 - who) }) {
            over!(("won", who))
          }
        | for (@pos <- r where pos |= @{ Stalemate(3 - who) }) {
            over!("draw")
          }
      }
    }

  | Boot!(Nil)
}
